f.write_text("# Dubon proof-reuse refresh — 17 November 2026\n\nThe actionable new find is the proved Dickson classification in Imperial College London's FLT repository. A bounded port now compiles on this project's unchanged Lean 4.30.0 / Mathlib revision and passes a full axiom audit and all sixteen declaration linters. Axiom's November 6 Weil-conjectures release is a second substantial candidate: its inspected geometric purity theorem could replace a large foundational proof, but its complete dependencies and the comparison with the actual Dubon modular form have not been verified locally.\n\nAcceptance remains **16/20; DUB-17–20 OPEN**. These findings do not change the frozen Dubon source, the full modular scope, the proof standards or the acceptance tests.\n\n| Source and pinned revision | What was checked | Use in the existing goal |\n|---|---|---|\n| [ImperialCollegeLondon/FLT](https://github.com/ImperialCollegeLondon/FLT/tree/478c1013cd29c27d72f57aa8b691fa46083664b3), `478c1013cd29c27d72f57aa8b691fa46083664b3` | The full fourteen-module FLT import closure of `DicksonClassification` was fetched. Its two admitted public classification declarations were omitted. The adapted thirteen-module closure, containing literal group/field objects and the twelve proof modules, compiles on the root pins. All 857 theorem declarations and 184 other declarations, including generated/private declarations, have only the permitted logical axioms. All sixteen linters pass in every module. | DUB-17 Theorem 2.6 / DUB-18 Corollary 8.2 → symmetric-power continuation → automorphy → actual original residual large image → finite projective-image classification. Reuse discharges the classification theorem; it does not exclude cyclic, dihedral, exceptional or solvable arithmetic images. The subsequent audited local adaptation below supplies the literal matrix and finite-coefficient comparisons. |\n| [AxiomMath/Weil](https://github.com/AxiomMath/Weil/tree/27cdb2049be62ee2911429b47f54774078450e32), `27cdb2049be62ee2911429b47f54774078450e32` | Fresh HEAD, complete repository path tree, three endpoints, their immediate Weil imports, and selected further definitions were downloaded. The repository contains 980 Lean modules and targets Lean 4.34.0-rc2 / Mathlib `16edf57de7933555826307a3d81b4391a3657a09`. `Weil.satisfiesWeilEstimate` is stated for actual smooth proper schemes over finite fields; its conclusion describes the magnitudes of geometric Frobenius eigenvalues. This is a source inspection, not a local build or full dependency audit. | Potential upstream aid if the selected image or automorphy route needs independent geometric purity. The existing `primitive_satoTate_of_entire_symmetric_continuation` already derives purity and boundary nonvanishing from the actual entire symmetric family, so a separate Weil port is not an additional requirement of that analytic criterion. The source modular form must still be realized in the appropriate cohomology with the exact Hecke/Frobenius, weight and embedding comparisons. No direct higher symmetric-power continuation or modular Sato–Tate endpoint was verified in this scope. |\n| [AxiomMath/Stacks](https://github.com/AxiomMath/Stacks/tree/012108f61d7f92a6ffc35ec3f263b6da5d438125), `012108f61d7f92a6ffc35ec3f263b6da5d438125` | The exact dependency revision pinned by Weil, its package metadata and selected purity-reduction sources were inspected. The intermediate `weightBound_of_inputs` has explicit Laumon, duality and reduction inputs; it is not itself an unconditional substitute. The reviewed geometric-component reduction does prove its named reduction input. Some inspected Weil comparison proofs raise `maxRecDepth`; those settings are incompatible with this project's port standard and have not been adopted. | Any selected purity route must include the complete proof closure discharging those inputs and compile under this project's ordinary limits. Rebuilding an unrelated general geometry library is deferred until the exact original-form comparison and shortest required closure are identified. |\n| [anthropics/fermats-last-theorem](https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/tree/6e837e75355538c7f80bab5b956861e86c4eacc2), `6e837e75355538c7f80bab5b956861e86c4eacc2` | A fresh API HEAD check equals the previously inspected revision. README, proof-path and package pins were downloaded again. Existing native ports and their audits remain the starting point; the whole external repository was not rebuilt. | No new revision was available to replace work already proved in this repository. Continue using the actual original trace, arithmetic descent, integral lattice, fixed-local Hecke and global determinant theorems already constructed here. |\n| [CICM24_Deligne](https://github.com/mkaratarakis/CICM24_Deligne/tree/8e5b163b30e434e6f68549e9399650923b75fb60), `8e5b163b30e434e6f68549e9399650923b75fb60` | Fresh pinned statement, Galois-representation and Frobenius sources were inspected. The Deligne endpoint remains admitted. | Useful statement/reference material only; it cannot discharge a required theorem. |\n\n## Adaptation and verification boundaries\n\nThe Dickson port retains Duxing Yang's Apache-2.0 notices and source provenance. It uses the native `Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Action` module, adapts renamed APIs, removes all upstream file-level options and per-declaration `nolint` attributes, removes four unused finiteness hypotheses from helper definitions, and confines a finite enumeration instance to the proof scopes that need it. No theorem's classification alternatives, prime range or group-isomorphism conclusions were weakened. Initial compatibility passes were not accepted as linter passes; the failed full audit and corrected successor are preserved separately.\n\nThe generic residual-image bridge constructs the entrywise coefficient map and projective range and proves their finiteness before applying Dickson. The next original-form consumer uses the actual integral/residual arithmetic action returned by `exists_gammaOne_original_fixedDeterminant_residual`; it supplies no representation or large-image hypothesis. Both the expanded conclusion and its equivalent named-predicate form hit the default elaboration limit. Both failures are preserved. A fresh decomposition separates the generic coefficient-map/classification witness from the original-form assembly, without raising limits or changing the conclusion. The decomposed original-form consumer now passes compilation and the complete scratch audit: 406 public axiom queries, 873 exhaustive theorem checks and 191 other declarations, all sixteen linters in fifteen modules, exact ownership enumeration, and sixteen required proof-value dependencies. Three generated instance names were reconciled with the canonical module environment. The arithmetic dependency check was corrected to require the local-coefficient theorem actually consumed by the residual construction, rather than its parallel existential wrapper. Both failed audits and their passing successors are preserved; no mathematical proof or assumption was weakened.\n\nThe source chain still requires arithmetic exclusion of the unwanted projective cases, remaining local/regularity hypotheses, automorphy and literal higher symmetric-power continuation. The subsequent matrix and finite-coefficient comparison below removes those two comparison premises. The later symmetric-power extension removes irreducibility premises on the large-image branch, not the arithmetic obligation of reaching that branch. The reviewed explicit large-image literature uses Deligne in a dihedral bound. Purity obtained from the still-unproved higher continuation cannot be used circularly to justify an upstream image argument for that continuation; that route would need independent purity or a different rigorous exclusion argument. Abstract PSL₂/PGL₂ isomorphism is weaker than a full SL₂ image over the specific residue field required by existing consumers.\n\n## Search scope and evidence\n\nThe November 17 searches targeted Lean proofs of residual Galois large image, Dickson classification, Deligne/Ramanujan–Petersson, modular Sato–Tate, and symmetric-power automorphy. Primary repository sources and exact theorem signatures take precedence over search summaries or announcements. No complete directly reusable missing Sato–Tate or higher symmetric-power endpoint was verified in the inspected results; that is a bounded finding, not a claim that none exists anywhere.\n\nEvidence prepared in the scratch workspace includes `flt-pgl-classification-20261117-2264/review.json`, `SourceDicksonPortAudit2287b.log`, `SourceDicksonPortDeclarations2291a.log`, `weil-reuse-refresh-20261117-2288/review.json`, `weil-stacks-flt-followup-20261117-2290/review.json` and the previously archived CICM follow-up. Public downloads are inert text; no downloaded scripts were executed. Strict source-consumer proof-value verification now passes. The 28-module scope has now been integrated. Its production aggregate, complete axiom/linter audit and both proof-value audits pass. Exact semantic reconciliation now passes for all 1,149 declarations, and the sequential foundation verification passes. Paper verification and the final global ownership check are still running; canonical acceptance remains pending.\n\nThe pinned text, tested adaptations, failed and passing audit logs, and hashes are retained in [the research evidence manifest](Tools/proof-reuse-refresh-20261117-2298/manifest.json). The [previous expanded review](Dubon%20Expanded%20Reuse%20Assessment%202026-11-16.md) remains historical evidence.\n\nThe original-form classification, its exact canonical sources, all failed and passing audit attempts, declaration enumeration, compile evidence and receipt are retained in [the scratch classification manifest](Tools/projective-classification-scratch-20261117-2308/manifest.json). Subsequent explicit matrix-conjugacy, linear-lifting and finite-descent extensions have their own passing scratch evidence linked below; the fifteen-module receipt does not cover those later extensions.\n\n## Subsequent arithmetic-route check\n\nThe pinned Anthropic FLT sources contain substantive Ribet irreducibility proofs. Eight proof files and their eight immediate definition imports were inspected. Their principal endpoint concerns characteristic-zero irreducibility of weight-two Hecke representations with Frobenius determinant `l`; the inertia comparison assumes an actual finite-flat model and first cyclotomic determinant. These statements do not directly supply residual irreducibility or the required local weights for the full original Dubon domain. The full theorem closures were not audited or ported, and some proofs raise heartbeat limits. They remain candidates for a justified adaptation, with no narrowing to weight two. The exact findings and the already-proved native continuation-to-purity route are retained in [the arithmetic route review](Tools/ribet-image-reuse-review-20261117-2314/manifest.json).\n\n## Actual matrix and finite-coefficient comparison\n\nThe original-form consumer now also proves explicit projective matrix conjugacy in the PSL₂/PGL₂ branches. The native perfect-SL₂ theorem lifts projective containment to a literal SL₂ subfield lower bound, while projective containment in PGL₂ supplies a scalar-times-GL₂ upper bound over the same subfield. Adjoining only the original finite coefficient image, that finite subfield and the conjugating matrix entries gives an actual finite intermediate field. Both embeddings and the change-of-basis matrix descend, and both linear bounds descend with exact coefficient-map compatibility.\n\nThe final original-form theorem retains the full projective classification, integral reduction, fixed-local Hecke/inertia data, global determinant and oddness, together with an explicit small-projective-image alternative or those finite-field linear bounds. No image or representation certificate is supplied. The 28-module scratch closure passes 436 public queries, all 939 theorem and 210 other declaration axiom checks, sixteen linters per module, exact ownership enumeration, and the final 45-edge proof-value audit. The intermediate consumer also retains its independent 36-edge check. [The complete scratch evidence](Tools/projective-linear-finite-scratch-20261117-2325/manifest.json) preserves failed attempts and fresh passing successors. Production integration is complete and the aggregate and focused checks pass. Full assembled verification remains pending.\n\nThis removes the abstract-isomorphism, central-scalar and finite-ambient-field comparison obstacles. It does not exclude cyclic, dihedral, A₄, S₄, A₅ or elementary-abelian-by-cyclic arithmetic images, supply the required original local deformation/regularity conditions, or prove automorphy and higher continuation. The full source scope and all acceptance gates remain unchanged.\n\n## Symmetric-power consumers and current verification\n\nThe previously assembled 9,697-module scope passed sequential foundation and paper verification, all thirteen regressions, the complete 60,975-theorem audit and all sixteen linters. Its immutable evidence is recorded in [the canonical rank-two receipt](Tools/original-rank-two-residual-20261117-2268/canonical-evidence-9697.json).\n\nFor the 28-module Dickson/finite-image integration, focused ownership reconciliation found one extra generated theorem, `Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.mkOfDetNeZero.congr_simp`. Both final-consumer environments assign it to Mathlib's Moebius-action module; the focused production import order assigns the identical theorem to the PGL helper. Its exact type and proof-value hashes agree and its axioms were checked independently. The failed comparison is preserved, and the reviewed successor explicitly accounts for this declaration. No mathematical source or proof was changed.\n\nA further native reuse closes the proper-subfield irreducibility gap on the large-image branch. The existing three-operator invariant-subspace proof now works for an embedded finite SL₂ subfield without identifying it with the ambient coefficient field. For each symmetric degree with `2*n < p-1`, one finite coefficient extension and one basis give irreducibility over every coefficient extension. The actual-form consumer retains all original integral, Frobenius, determinant and oddness data. Its four-module scratch closure passes all public and private/generated axiom checks (14 theorem and 5 other declarations), all sixteen linters, exact ownership enumeration and a 50-edge proof-value audit.\n\nThe existing perfect-subgroup character-kernel lemma further retains that SL₂ image on the original arithmetic cyclotomic kernel. The combined seven-module scope passes its exhaustive audit (29 theorem and 10 other declarations), all sixteen linters, exact ownership enumeration and 55 proof-value edges. The native surjective absolute-to-arithmetic restriction then retains the bounds on the actual absolute Galois cyclotomic kernel. This nine-module scope also passes: 39 theorem and 15 other declarations, ten public queries, all sixteen linters, exact ownership enumeration and 59 proof-value edges.\n\nA further short native route removes the auxiliary coefficient field and basis from the required irreducibility conclusion. The proved representation-equivalence API transfers irreducibility across literal conjugation; native Burnside and coefficient descent give full matrix spanning over the original residue field. Matrix units preserve that spanning under every original coefficient extension. Mathlib's existing simplicity theorem for modules over their full endomorphism ring then gives absolute irreducibility in the original coordinates. The actual-form consumer `exists_gammaOne_original_residue_absolute_symmetric_irreducibility` compiles and proves this on both original arithmetic and absolute cyclotomic kernels, for every degree `n` with `2*n < p-1`, on the large-image branch. It retains the original integral reduction, Hecke/Frobenius, determinant, oddness and complete projective alternatives. No irreducibility, representation, field-comparison or change-of-basis premise is supplied by the caller.\n\nThe eighteen-module combined scratch scope is undergoing its exhaustive axiom, sixteen-linter, ownership and proof-value audits. Failed elaboration attempts are retained. The successful original-field source consumers apply the already-proved finite-image consumer directly; the earlier longer finite-extension consumers remain separate proved declarations and are included as a second source root in the dependency audit. No accepted mathematical body, prime range or source scope was weakened. These scratch results await their own completed verification and canonical integration.\n\nThe exact outstanding chain remains DUB-17 Theorem 2.6 / DUB-18 Corollary 8.2 → literal higher symmetric-power continuation → automorphy → actual residual image and local conditions. This work discharges the original absolute irreducibility input on the large-image branch. Arithmetic small-image exclusions, the required local conditions, automorphy and higher continuation remain open; acceptance stays 16/20.\n",encoding="utf-8")
